<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Commonwealth of England</h1><p class="st-meta">1649 CE – 1660 CE · capital London</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The republic that replaced the monarchy after the execution of Charles I in 1649, the only period without a king in English history.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Parliament's victory in the Civil Wars, Charles I was tried and executed in January 1649. The Rump Parliament abolished the monarchy and the House of Lords and declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> a Commonwealth governed without a king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Cromwell</a> then conquered Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, which were joined to it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Power lay with the Rump, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-council-of-state">Council of State</a> and the New Model Army. The regime passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navigation-acts">Navigation Act</a> of 1651, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-dutch-war">First Anglo-Dutch War</a> and allowed some religious toleration while repressing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levellers">Levellers</a> and royalists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Cromwell</a> expelled the Rump in 1653, and the nominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barebones-parliament">Barebone's Parliament</a> failed, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> collapsed in 1659 the restored Rump briefly revived the Commonwealth. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-monck">George Monck</a> marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-parliament-1660">Convention Parliament</a> invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II</a> back, and the monarchy was restored in May 1660 in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/commonwealth-of-england.jpg" alt="Commonwealth of England" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Richtom80 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_The_Commonwealth.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1649 CE – 1660 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>London</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Oliver Cromwell, John Bradshaw</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestant Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom, Ireland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>English Commonwealth, Commonwealth of England, Scotland and Ireland, Interregnum</dd></dl></aside></div>
